  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nA few light showers may be possible, mainly in the northern half of \nthe forecast area, as an upper level trough propels a reinforcing \ncold front across the area today. Precipitation should then become \nconfined to upslope showers or drizzle in the northeast mountains as \nthe upper trough continues to pivot overhead tonight into Saturday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nCold air advection behind today's front is expected to send low \ntemperatures into the near to below normal range tonight. While \nfrost isn't out of the question, residual cloud cover could prevent \ntemperatures from cooling enough for development in the counties \nwhere the growing season remains active (near and south of I-64).\n\nTemperatures will remain seasonably chilly as high pressure builds \nin behind the upper trough during the weekend and early next week. \nConfidence in frost/freeze is highest for early Sunday and Monday \nmornings, then temperatures may moderate slightly towards mid-week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nShortwaves moving through a larger upper trough are expected to \nbring precipitation back into the area late Wednesday into Thursday, \nwith cold air and an active pattern projected to persist through the \nend of the week.",
